==German==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gmh|de}} {{m|gmh|beginnen}}, from {{etyl|goh|de}} {{m|goh|biginnan}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|de}} {{m|gem-pro|*biginnaną}}. Cognate with Low German {{m|nds|beginnen}}, Dutch {{m|nl|beginnen}}, West Frisian {{m|fy|begjinne}}, and English {{m|en|begin}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/bəˈɡɪnən/|[bəˈɡɪnən]|[bəˈɡɪnn̩]|lang=de}}
* {{audio|De-beginnen.ogg|audio (Germany)|lang=de}}
* {{audio|De-at-beginnen.ogg|audio (Austria)|lang=de}}

===Verb===
{{de-verb-strong|class=3|beginnt|begann|begonnen}}

# {{label|de|intransitive}} to [[begin]]; to [[commence]]; to be started
#: ''Der Vortrag hat '''begonnen'''.'' — “The lecture has '''begun'''.”
# {{label|de|chiefly|literary|transitive|_|or with|_|{{l|de|mit}}}} to [[start]] something; to [[begin]] something
#: ''Er hat den Vortrag '''begonnen.''''' — “He has '''started''' the lecture.”
#: ''Er hat mit dem Vortrag '''begonnen.''''' — “He has '''started''' the lecture.”

====Usage notes====
* ''Beginnen'' is relatively rare in colloquial German and is most often replaced with {{m|de|anfangen}}. This is particularly true of the sense “to start something”.
* In literary German, ''beginnen'' is often preferred to ''anfangen'', though both are perfectly acceptable.

====Related terms====
* {{l|de|Beginn}}

====Conjugation====
{{de-conj-strong|beginn|begann|begonnen|h|||begänn|7b=begönn|a}}

